//// `print` is std's `io.terminal.print` (decision 606 (2)): a file that does not import it
//// has no `print`, and the refusal names the import.
////
//// The gap this pins (`00 · 01-checker`, `00 · 02-erlang` / `11-tooling`):
//// `print` and `println` were bound as placeholders, so the call type-checked
//// and only commonJS lowered it — erlang emitted a call to an undefined local
//// (`erlc` refused the module, and a library module holding one was skipped
//// by the test runner's loader), beam an unresolved call, wasm a trap.

fn shout(line: string) -> string {
    print(line);
    return line;
}

pub fn main() {
    shout("hi");
}
